The Lost key
Anna was feeling nervous. She had a very important job interview in the 
city center at 10:00 AM. It was now 8:30, and she was ready to leave her 
small apartment.

She put on her best blue jacket, picked up her bag, and loocked for her keys
to lock the door. They were not on the table where she usually left them.
She checked her pockets. Nothing.

"Where are they?" she thought, starting to worry. She looked in the kitchen,
the bathroom, and under the sofa. No keys.

Her phone rang. It was her friend, Leo. "Anna, are you on your way? The traffic is bad today," he said.

"I cant find my keys!" Anna replied, her voice tense.

"Calm down," Leo said. "Think. When did you last use them?"

Anna took a deep breath. She remembered comming home from the
supermarket last night. She was carrying heavy bags. "I think... I think I left
them in the door!" she exclaimed.

She ran to the front door and opened it. There, hanging from the outside
lock, were her keys. She felt relieved but also silly. "Found them!" she told
Leo. "They were outside all night!"

"That was lucky no one took them," Leo laughed. "Now hurry up!"

Anna locked the door carefully, double-checked she had the keys, and ran
to the bus stop. The bus was just arriving. She got on, found a seat, and relaxed a litle. She had enough time.

During the bus ride, she thought about her interview. She really wanted this job at the library. She loved books and quiet places. She qualified and
had practiced her answers many times.

She arrived at he office building with fifteen minutes to spare. She entered
the lobby and toched her pocket to feel her keys for confidence. Her 
pocket was empty. Her heart stopped.

Her keys were not in her pocket. They were not in her bag. She must have
dropped them on the bus!

Panic returned. Her apartament key, her bike key, abs a special keychain
from her grandmother were on that ring. The interview was in ten minutes,
but she couldnt focus.

She saw a security guard at the desk. She decided to ask for help. "Excuse 
me", she said, trying to sound calm. "I think I lost my keys on the number 57
but. Is there a lost and found office here?"

The guard, a kind older man, smiled. "Not here, but you can call the bus
company later. Dont worry. People hand in lost items all the time."

His words made her feel a litle better. She thacked him and went to her
interview. She tried to forget about the keys and concentrate.

The interview went well. The manager liked her experience and her passion
for books. "Well call you next week," she said.

Anna left the building. She didnt know if she got job, and she had lost
her keys. It was not a perfect morning. She called the bus company and 
reported her lost keys. They told her to call back in the afternoon.

Feeling tired, she took the bus home. When she got to her apartament door,
she remembered she couldnt get in. She sat on the stairs, thinking about
what to do.

Just them, her neighbor, Mr. Davies, came out. "Hello, Anna! Everything 
okay" he asked.

Anna had completely forgotten! She was so grateful. "Thank you so much,
Mr. Davies! You saved my day."

Late that afternoon, the bus company called. A driver had found her keys 
on his bus! She could collect them the next day.

That enening, Anna felt happy. Her keys were safe, she had a spare now,
and her interview went well. Sometimes, she thought, a bad start can still
have a happy ending.
